From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noite <noite@noite.now>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 23:40:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] rpc: stubs and byte streams cross isolates (same node)

Void's sandbox client runs in a Worker and drives a container in a
Durable Object: the exec call returns {pid,isPty,stdout,stderr,stdin,
capability}, then reads both byte streams, writes stdin, and awaits
exitCode on the capability. Upstream celld lifts those values into
same-isolate stub markers that fail on use across isolates, so the
whole shape arrives dead.

Mint every Durable Object RPC export under a host-routable id instead:
a process-wide stub table keyed by 128-bit CSPRNG ids, recording the
owning cell scope, activation epoch, application generation, kind, and
the holder invocation/isolate the id was delivered to. Stub operations
validate id shape, scope, epoch, generation, residency, and caller
binding against the table on every op (before routing and after the
pin closes the eviction race), then drive a CellJob::StubOp on the
owner's isolate, where the existing __rpcWalk runs the method/property
path and bridge entries serve stream reads, writes, cancels, closes,
and aborts. Replies gate exactly like RPC replies.

Lifetime is Cloudflare-like invocation ownership: a stub received by a
caller belongs to its fetch/RPC/alarm/queue invocation, which disposes
it when the invocation ends (including ctx.abort and isolate
retirement) and routes the release to the owner. In-flight ops pin
their entries; there is no idle-timeout reclaim while the holding
invocation is alive. The only backstop is for provably gone holders
(retired heaps), never a wall-clock sweep of live work.

Worker-owned arguments cross by push: a ReadableStream (or the
abort-signal carrier Void passes) in call arguments is pumped
caller-side into a bounded, backpressured host channel the cell pulls,
with cancellation propagating both ways; AbortSignal rides the same
live mechanism. Caller-owned WritableStream has no reverse path and
keeps its same-isolate marker. Caps (per-scope and per-process) and
strict id/string validation fail closed; collision retries never
reroute a victim. Remote owners reject with a clear cross-node error;
service-binding stubs keep failing as before; old-shape markers revive
exactly as before.

Tests: stub_table (21) and upload_table (15) unit tests plus a
rewritten rpc_stub_conformance black-box suite (Void-shaped exec with
signal and long quiet stream, request-end disposal asserting owner and
JS state, live-holder longevity without wall sleeps, caps, forged
markers refused at lift, post-end revives arriving disposed, stats
hook gated by env, bounded release requeue, concurrent drives keeping
sibling revives, self-fetch capabilities usable, throwing entrypoint
op ending its ctx), green in ~70 s.
